+D1–D5 are the original defects. **D6–D9 were added during the Meta hackathon (2026-10-27)**
+because Muse Spark 1.3 fixed D1–D5 in a single shot. Each `defect.json` has a `trap` field explaining
+why it is designed to defeat a one-shot repair:
+
+* **D6** has two causes; the failing assertion only reports the first, so a one-shot fix of the
+  amount still fails on the restock race.
+* **D7**'s quickest fix (bypassing the cache) breaks a rate-limit contract tested in a *different*
+  file (`test/pricing-cache.test.ts`), which the one-shot baseline never sees.
+* **D8**'s cause is a runtime property of the regex object shared by every caller; patching one call
+  site passes the visible test but fails the hidden ones.
+* **D9** passes in isolation and fails only after an earlier test in its file: FORGE-0's flaky-test
+  triage classifies it as *order-dependent* before any hypothesis is formed.
+
+**D10–D12 were added next**, after both the edit-test loop and FORGE-0 fixed D1–D9 every time. They
+were [pre-registered](../../benchmarks/preregistration/2026-10-27-harder-defects.md) before any run:
+in each, the root cause is a *runtime* fact that the code in the prompt does not show, and a local
+patch passes every visible test while leaving it in place.
+
+* **D10**: the fees are strings in a JSON file read at runtime; converting the one fee in the failing
+  path leaves the gift-wrap fee broken.
+* **D11**: de-duplicating loyalty awards hides the symptom, but receipts are still duplicated and the
+  subscriber list keeps growing.
+* **D12**: sorting `lines()` by an insertion sequence leaves checkout listing products by id.
 
 Each `defect.json` contains the description, relevant files, reproduction steps, failing test,
 expected behaviour and regression tests. Each `reference-fix.json` contains search/replace edits in
 the same format FORGE-0 uses.
 
